------------------------------ Observables with RxJS ------------------------------------------------

- LEARN MORE ABOUT REACTIVE PROGRAMMING AND USE OBSERVABLES IN THE SERVICE.

- Instruction: Angular Prerequisite "Reactive Programming" chapter

1️⃣ 🔥🔥Eventually, the race service will get the races from the server. This request will use the
    HTTP client provided by Angular and, as any AJAX request, it will be asynchronous. The result
    will thus not be immediately available, and can’t thus be returned directly by the method.
    🔥🔥 Instead, an Observable will be returned, and the component will have to subscribe to
    this Observable.

    Transform the service to make it return an Observable instead of an array. 🔥🔥Wrapping a
    value into an Observable is easy, using the of function.

        🔥🔥🔥Note: of([1, 2, 3, 4])
        This is different from the from function. of will only emit one event which will be the
        complete array ([1, 2, 3, 4] in the previous example). from would emit one event per item
        of the array (1, then 2 then 3, then 4). Here we want to use of because, in the long run,
        this will be replaced by an HTTP request which will return an array of races in one request.
    
    🔥🔥But of() will return right away. In real life, the results will take some time to fetch,
    so let’s add an operator called delay, to…​ delay the results for half a second.

        export class RaceService {
            list(): Observable<Array<RaceModel>> {
                return of([....]).pipe(delay(500));
            }
        }
    

 2️⃣ modify the Races component. 🔥🔥It must now subscribe to the Observable to get the list of races.
    The cleanest way to do this is to use toSignal!

        protected readonly races = toSignal(inject(RaceService).list());

    Previously we had races as a simple array so in the template we used @for (race of races; ...)
    But now races is a signal so template should be @for (race of races(); ...)


